.SH DESCRIPTION
The 32-bit engine's concurrent surface, present in every 32-bit
.B libexpanse
(std or not) and absent from 64-bit builds, which carry
.BR expanse_sync (3)
instead. Declared inside
.B #if !EXPANSE_WIDE_SURFACE
in
.IR expanse.h ,
which also defines
.B EXPANSE_HAS_SYNC32
for capability tests.
.PP
.B Protocol.
Blocking optimistic lock coupling, single attempt, no mutex fallback \(em
not lock-free. The writer brackets every mutation with a version bump; a
reader samples the version, walks, and validates. A read that overlaps an
open bracket, or observes torn content, returns
.B EXPANSE_SYNC32_BUSY
immediately and never spins: on a single-core part, BUSY inside an
interrupt handler means the handler preempted the writer inside its
bracket, and the writer cannot progress until the handler returns. Surface
BUSY and retry on the next invocation. The wrapper is atomic load/store
plus fences only \(em no compare-and-swap, which the riscv32imc parts lack.
.PP
.B Memory.
Fixed capacity by design.
.I node_cap
node slots and
.I max_readers
reader slots (64 bytes each) are reserved by
.B _new
and never grow. A mutation needing more than
.BR expanse_sync32_mutation_headroom ()
free slots is refused with
.BR EXPANSE_SYNC32_ARENA_FULL ;
one that cannot reclaim because a reader is inside a walk is refused with
.BR EXPANSE_SYNC32_RECLAIM_BACKLOG .
A refused mutation leaves the tree untouched. Freed nodes are parked until
every reader is observed outside a walk; that wait is not time-bounded.
Writer calls allocate (a bracket may contain up to the headroom's worth of
allocator calls) and free; reader
.B try_*
calls never allocate and never block.
.PP
.B Handles and execution contexts.
The writer handle is one per container, used from one execution context at
a time, not reentrant, and never used from an interrupt handler. A reader
handle is one per execution context: a main loop and the interrupt handler
that preempts it use different indices \(em sharing one is a
use-after-free in waiting (the inner walk's exit unpins the outer walk).
Reader
.B try_*
calls are the only interrupt-safe entry points. Handles are owned by the
container and dangle after
.BR _free ,
which must run with reader interrupts masked, reader tasks joined, and no
writer call in progress. The contract holds within one hart; readers on
another core are not supported by this version.
.PP
.B Status codes
come in three bands: outcomes
.RB ( OK "=0, " NOT_FOUND "=1, " BUSY =2),
refusals that leave the tree untouched
.RB ( ARENA_FULL "=16, " RECLAIM_BACKLOG =17),
and usage errors asserted in debug builds
.RB ( NULL_HANDLE =32).
Each function's header comment lists the values it can return;
.BR expanse_sync32_status_str ()
names any value. New values may be added within a band.
.TP
.BI "expanse_sync32_map_t *expanse_sync32_map_new(size_t " node_cap ","
.BI "                                             size_t " max_readers ");"
Returns NULL if
.I node_cap
is below the headroom (or on an allocation abort path).
.TP
.BI "expanse_sync32_map_writer_t *expanse_sync32_map_writer("
.BI "        expanse_sync32_map_t *" map ");"
.TQ
.BI "expanse_sync32_map_reader_t *expanse_sync32_map_reader("
.BI "        expanse_sync32_map_t *" map ", size_t " idx ");"
Idempotent accessors: the same pointer every call, no allocation, no
atomics. NULL for a NULL container or an out-of-range index.
.TP
.BI "expanse_sync32_map_writer_stats(" w ", " stats ", " stats_size ")"
Fills the first
.I stats_size
bytes of an append-only struct: pass
.BR sizeof(*stats) .
.TP
.BI "expanse_sync32_map_writer_try_remove(" w ", " key ", " old_out ")"
Headroom is checked before the lookup, so an absent key can still be
refused with a capacity status.
